## Proof

We prove: if x, y ≥ 1 are integers such that for all n ≥ 1, the set of primes dividing x^n − 1 equals the set of primes dividing y^n − 1, then x = y.

### Case 1: x = 1 or y = 1

If x = 1, then x^n − 1 = 0 for all n, so every prime divides x^n − 1. The condition requires every prime to divide y^n − 1 for all n. Since a nonzero integer has finitely many prime divisors, we must have y^n − 1 = 0, hence y = 1. By symmetry, y = 1 implies x = 1. So x = y = 1. ✓

### Case 2: x, y ≥ 2

**Step 1: rad(x) = rad(y) (same prime factors).**

Suppose p | x. Then x^n ≡ 0 (mod p), so x^n − 1 ≡ −1 (mod p), hence p ∤ x^n − 1 for all n. If p ∤ y, then ord_p(y) is well-defined and p | y^{ord_p(y)} − 1. Setting n = ord_p(y), we get p | y^n − 1 but p ∤ x^n − 1, contradicting the hypothesis. So p | y. By symmetry, every prime dividing y divides x. Hence rad(x) = rad(y).

**Step 2: ord_p(x) = ord_p(y) for all primes p ∤ xy.**

For such p, the hypothesis gives: for all n ≥ 1, (ord_p(x) | n) ⟺ (ord_p(y) | n). Taking n = ord_p(x) yields ord_p(y) | ord_p(x); taking n = ord_p(y) yields ord_p(x) | ord_p(y). So ord_p(x) = ord_p(y).

**Step 3: x and y are multiplicatively dependent.**

Since (ℤ/pℤ)* is cyclic for prime p, there is a unique subgroup of each order. Thus ord_p(x) = ord_p(y) implies ⟨x mod p⟩ = ⟨y mod p⟩ in (ℤ/pℤ)* for every prime p ∤ xy. Equivalently, for all primes p ∤ xy, y ≡ x^{j_p} (mod p) for some j_p.

This implies: for all n ≥ 2 and all primes p ≡ 1 (mod n) with p ∤ xy, x is an n-th power residue mod p if and only if y is. (Both lie in the same cyclic subgroup, so n-th power residuosity—equivalent to ord_p(·) | (p−1)/n—coincides.)

The primes p ≡ 1 (mod n) where x is an n-th power residue are exactly the primes splitting completely in the Kummer extension ℚ(ζ_n, x^{1/n}) (which is Galois over ℚ). By the Chebotarev density theorem, two Galois extensions of ℚ with the same set of splitting primes (up to finitely many) are equal. Therefore:

$$\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_n, x^{1/n}) = \mathbb{Q}(\zeta_n, y^{1/n}) \quad \text{for all } n \geq 2.$$

By Kummer theory over ℚ(ζ_n), this means x and y generate the same subgroup in ℚ(ζ_n)*/(ℚ(ζ_n)*)^n. In particular, for each n, there exists an integer j and w ∈ ℚ(ζ_n)* with y = x^j · w^n.

**Suppose for contradiction that x, y are multiplicatively independent.** Write x = ∏ q_i^{a_i}, y = ∏ q_i^{b_i} (same primes by Step 1). Independence means the exponent vectors (a_i), (b_i) are not proportional, so there exist q_1, q_2 with D := a_{q_1} b_{q_2} − a_{q_2} b_{q_1} ≠ 0.

Take n = ℓ a prime with ℓ > |D|, ℓ ∤ xy, and ℓ ∤ (a_{q_1} a_{q_2}). From y = x^j · w^ℓ, taking valuations at any prime ideal 𝔮 above q_i (using that q_i ∤ ℓ, so q_i is unramified and v_𝔮(x) = v_{q_i}(x) = a_{q_i}):

$$b_{q_i} \equiv j \cdot a_{q_i} \pmod{\ell}, \quad i = 1, 2.$$

Since ℓ ∤ a_{q_i}, we solve: j ≡ b_{q_1} a_{q_1}^{-1} ≡ b_{q_2} a_{q_2}^{-1} (mod ℓ), giving b_{q_1} a_{q_2} − b_{q_2} a_{q_1} ≡ 0 (mod ℓ), i.e., ℓ | D. But 0 < |D| < ℓ — contradiction.

Hence x, y are **multiplicatively dependent**: x^r = y^s for some positive integers r, s. Reducing to gcd(r, s) = 1, we get x = t^s, y = t^r for some integer t ≥ 2.

**Step 4: x = y.**

The hypothesis becomes: for all n ≥ 1, the prime divisors of t^{sn} − 1 and t^{rn} − 1 coincide.

Suppose s > r. Choose n so that m := sn ≥ 3 and (t, m) ≠ (2, 6) (always possible: if s ≥ 3 take n = 1 unless (t,s) = (2,6), in which case take n = 2; if s = 2 take n = 2 giving m = 4). By the **Bang–Zsygmondy theorem**, t^m − 1 has a primitive prime divisor p (p | t^m − 1 but p ∤ t^k − 1 for all 1 ≤ k < m). Since rn < sn = m, we have p ∤ t^{rn} − 1, yet p | t^{sn} − 1 — contradicting the hypothesis.

So s ≤ r. By symmetry (exchanging x and y), r ≤ s. Hence r = s, and gcd(r, s) = 1 forces r = s = 1, giving **x = y = t**. ✓

### Conclusion

In all cases, x = y. The answer is **True**. ∎

